(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("openstack",{"openstack/what-openstack-is-and-core-services":{"html":"\u003cp>OpenStack is open-source software that turns a rack of servers into a private cloud. Teams get virtual machines, networks and disks on demand through an API, much like a public cloud, but on hardware the company owns. It suits organisations that need to keep data in their own buildings, control cost at large scale, or follow rules about where workloads run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The core services and what each does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Control plane and compute nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The two things every service depends on: a database and a message queue\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How OpenStack names map to AWS\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-core-services\">The core services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Service\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Name\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Closest AWS idea\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Identity\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Keystone\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Users, projects, roles, tokens, service catalog\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>IAM\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Compute\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nova\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Creates and manages virtual machines\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EC2\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Networking\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Neutron\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Networks, subnets, routers, security groups, floating IPs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VPC\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Image\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Glance\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stores VM images\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>AMIs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Block storage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cinder\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Volumes that attach to VMs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>EBS\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object storage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Swift\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Objects over HTTP\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>S3\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Orchestration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Heat\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Templates that build stacks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CloudFormation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Dashboard\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Horizon\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Web interface\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Console\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Placement\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Placement\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tracks resources and picks hosts\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Part of the scheduler\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every service has a REST API, and all of them accept the same token from Keystone. The \u003ccode>openstack\u003c/code> command line client talks to those APIs, and so does the dashboard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-plane-and-compute\">Control plane and compute\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Controller nodes\u003c/strong> run the API services, schedulers, the database and the message queue. They are the \u003cstrong>control plane\u003c/strong>. Run three for high availability.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Compute nodes\u003c/strong> run the hypervisor (usually KVM through libvirt) and the Nova compute agent. This is where virtual machines run.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Storage nodes\u003c/strong> serve volumes (often Ceph) or objects.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Network nodes\u003c/strong> (or the compute nodes themselves) run the Neutron agents.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-they-all-depend-on\">What they all depend on\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two pieces of shared infrastructure sit under almost every service:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>database\u003c/strong> (MariaDB or MySQL) that stores state: which servers exist, which networks, which users.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>message queue\u003c/strong> (RabbitMQ) through which services send each other requests. When you ask Nova to boot a server, the API writes to the database and sends a message to the scheduler, which sends one to a compute node.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>If the queue or database is unhealthy, many services fail together, with strange symptoms. When many different services show errors at once, look at these two first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-first-request-looks-like\">What a first request looks like\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack token issue -c expires -c project_id\nopenstack server list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack token issue -c expires -c project_id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+------------+----------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Field      | Value                            |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+------------+----------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| expires    | 2026-11-03T18:00:00+0000         |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| project_id | 4b1c8a0f6d5e4f2bb0a1c2d3e4f5a6b7 |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+------------+----------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+--------------------------------------+--------+--------+---------------------+-------+----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| ID                                   | Name   | Status | Networks            | Image | Flavor   |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+--------------------------------------+--------+--------+---------------------+-------+----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| 3f2a...                              | web-1  | ACTIVE | private=10.0.0.12   | ubuntu| m1.small |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+--------------------------------------+--------+--------+---------------------+-------+----------+\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A successful \u003ccode>token issue\u003c/code> proves that Keystone works and your credentials are right. If that fails, nothing else will work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-choose-it-and-what-it-costs\">Why choose it, and what it costs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A private cloud gives control and predictable cost at scale. The price is operations: you run the control plane, plan upgrades every six months or so, and manage hardware. Many teams use a distribution or deployment tool (Kolla-Ansible, OpenStack-Ansible, vendor products) to reduce that work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-every-service-broke-at-once\">Incident: Every service broke at once\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Nova, Neutron and Cinder all reported errors within the same minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack compute service list\nsudo rabbitmqctl cluster_status | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| nova-conductor | ctl-1 | internal | enabled | down |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| nova-scheduler | ctl-1 | internal | enabled | down |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> rabbitmqctl cluster_status | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: unable to perform an operation on node 'rabbit@ctl-1'. Please see diagnostics information and suggestion below.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The services were healthy; the message queue on the controller had stopped after its disk filled with logs. Services could not talk to each other, so each reported its own error. Freeing the disk and restarting RabbitMQ brought everything back within minutes. The team added disk alerts for the queue and treats a burst of unrelated failures as a sign to look at shared dependencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Nova, Neutron and Cinder all report errors at the same time. Find the cause.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># restart each service one by one\nsudo systemctl restart nova-api neutron-server cinder-api\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ openstack compute service list\n| nova-conductor | ctl-1 | internal | enabled | down |\n$ sudo journalctl -u nova-conductor -n 2\nERROR oslo.messaging: AMQP server on ctl-1:5672 is unreachable\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> The services were never the fault. They all depend on the message queue, which had stopped. Restarting clients cannot fix a broken shared dependency, and it adds load while they retry.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>sudo rabbitmqctl cluster_status\ndf -h /var/lib/rabbitmq\nsudo systemctl restart rabbitmq-server\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Check the shared dependencies first (queue, database, disk), then restart the clients only if they stay down.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating each service as independent and ignoring the queue and database\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a single controller in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting public-cloud features (such as managed databases) to exist without installing them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping upgrades until the gap is too big\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-core-services","text":"The core services"},{"id":"control-plane-and-compute","text":"Control plane and compute"},{"id":"what-they-all-depend-on","text":"What they all depend on"},{"id":"what-a-first-request-looks-like","text":"What a first request looks like"},{"id":"why-choose-it-and-what-it-costs","text":"Why choose it, and what it costs"},{"id":"incident-every-service-broke-at-once","text":"Incident: Every service broke at once"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/"},{"t":"OpenStack: Install guide","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/install-guide/"},{"t":"OpenStack: Operations guide","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/operations-guide/"}],"text":"OpenStack is open-source software that turns a rack of servers into a private cloud. Teams get virtual machines, networks and disks on demand through an API, much like a public cloud, but on hardware the company owns. It suits organisations that need to keep data in their own buildings, control cost at large scale, or follow rules about where workloads run. What you will learn The core services and what each does Control plane and compute nodes The two things every service depends on: a database and a message queue How OpenStack names map to AWS The core services Service Name What it does Closest AWS idea Identity Keystone Users, projects, roles, tokens, service catalog IAM Compute Nova Creates and manages virtual machines EC2 Networking Neutron Networks, subnets, routers, security groups, floating IPs VPC Image Glance Stores VM images AMIs Block storage Cinder Volumes that attach to VMs EBS Object storage Swift Objects over HTTP S3 Orchestration Heat Templates that build stacks CloudFormation Dashboard Horizon Web interface Console Placement Placement Tracks resources and picks hosts Part of the scheduler Every service has a REST API, and all of them accept the same token from Keystone. The openstack command line client talks to those APIs, and so does the dashboard. Control plane and compute Controller nodes run the API services, schedulers, the database and the message queue. They are the control plane . Run three for high availability. Compute nodes run the hypervisor (usually KVM through libvirt) and the Nova compute agent. This is where virtual machines run. Storage nodes serve volumes (often Ceph) or objects. Network nodes (or the compute nodes themselves) run the Neutron agents. What they all depend on Two pieces of shared infrastructure sit under almost every service: A database (MariaDB or MySQL) that stores state: which servers exist, which networks, which users. A message queue (RabbitMQ) through which services send each other requests. When you ask Nova to boot a server, the API writes to the database and sends a message to the scheduler, which sends one to a compute node. If the queue or database is unhealthy, many services fail together, with strange symptoms. When many different services show errors at once, look at these two first. What a first request looks like $ openstack token issue -c expires -c project_id +------------+----------------------------------+ | Field | Value | +------------+----------------------------------+ | expires | 2026-11-03T18:00:00+0000 | | project_id | 4b1c8a0f6d5e4f2bb0a1c2d3e4f5a6b7 | +------------+----------------------------------+ $ openstack server list +--------------------------------------+--------+--------+---------------------+-------+----------+ | ID | Name | Status | Networks | Image | Flavor | +--------------------------------------+--------+--------+---------------------+-------+----------+ | 3f2a... | web-1 | ACTIVE | private=10.0.0.12 | ubuntu| m1.small | +--------------------------------------+--------+--------+---------------------+-------+----------+ A successful token issue proves that Keystone works and your credentials are right. If that fails, nothing else will work. Why choose it, and what it costs A private cloud gives control and predictable cost at scale. The price is operations: you run the control plane, plan upgrades every six months or so, and manage hardware. Many teams use a distribution or deployment tool (Kolla-Ansible, OpenStack-Ansible, vendor products) to reduce that work. Incident: Every service broke at once Incident Nova, Neutron and Cinder all reported errors within the same minute. $ openstack compute service list | nova-conductor | ctl-1 | internal | enabled | down | | nova-scheduler | ctl-1 | internal | enabled | down | $ sudo rabbitmqctl cluster_status | head -3 Error: unable to perform an operation on node 'rabbit@ctl-1'. Please see diagnostics information and suggestion below. The services were healthy; the message queue on the contro"},"openstack/identity-projects-quotas-and-keystone":{"html":"\u003cp>Before anything is created in OpenStack, someone has to prove who they are and say which project the work belongs to. Keystone handles that.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Domains, projects, users and roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tokens, the service catalog and application credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quotas and how a project hits them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading common authentication errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-model\">The model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>domain\u003c/strong> groups users and projects, which lets one cloud serve several organisations.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>project\u003c/strong> (older name: tenant) owns resources such as servers and networks, and quotas are applied to it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>user\u003c/strong> is a person or a service account.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>role\u003c/strong> is a name such as \u003ccode>admin\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>member\u003c/code>. A user gets a role \u003cstrong>on a project\u003c/strong>. The same user can be admin on one project and a plain member on another.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Permissions are decided by policy files in each service, which map roles to actions. By default, members can manage resources in their project, and \u003ccode>admin\u003c/code> can manage the whole cloud.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tokens-and-the-catalog\">Tokens and the catalog\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You sign in and receive a \u003cstrong>token\u003c/strong> (a temporary credential), which you present to every service. The token is scoped to one project. The \u003cstrong>service catalog\u003c/strong> returned with it lists the API endpoints, so the client knows where Nova and Neutron live.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"env | grep ^OS_\nopenstack catalog list -c Name -c Type\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> | grep ^OS_\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OS_AUTH_URL=https://cloud.example.com:5000/v3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OS_PROJECT_NAME=shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OS_USERNAME=asha\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OS_USER_DOMAIN_NAME=Default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OS_PROJECT_DOMAIN_NAME=Default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack catalog list -c Name -c Type\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Name     | Type     |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| keystone | identity |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| nova     | compute  |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| neutron  | network  |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+----------+\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For automation, prefer \u003cstrong>application credentials\u003c/strong>: a secret limited to one project and, if you choose, to certain roles, that can be revoked without changing a person&#39;s password.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quotas\">Quotas\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each project has limits on instances, vCPUs, RAM, volumes, floating IPs, security groups and more. They stop one team from using the whole cloud.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack quota show -c cores -c instances -c ram\nopenstack server create --flavor m1.xlarge --image ubuntu app-9\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack quota show -c cores -c instances -c ram\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+-----------+-------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Resource  | Limit |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+-----------+-------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| cores     | 20    |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| instances | 10    |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| ram       | 51200 |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+-----------+-------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server create --flavor m1.xlarge --image ubuntu app-9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Quota exceeded for cores: Requested 8, but already used 16 of 20 cores (HTTP 403)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A quota error is not a fault in the cloud: it is a rule. The remedies are to delete unused resources, use a smaller flavor, or ask an admin to raise the quota. Raising quotas for everyone to avoid the question removes the protection.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-authentication-errors\">Reading authentication errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>HTTP 401 Unauthorized\u003c/strong>: the token or password is wrong or expired. Check the \u003ccode>OS_\u003c/code> variables and the domain names. A password right for one domain fails in another.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>HTTP 403 Forbidden\u003c/strong>: you are authenticated but the policy denies the action for your role. Ask for the right role on the project.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Could not find project\u003c/strong>: the project name is wrong, or the project is in a different domain.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Clock skew\u003c/strong>: tokens carry timestamps. A server whose clock is far off will reject valid tokens. Keep NTP running on every node.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-everything-worked-yesterday\">Incident: Everything worked yesterday\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment script began failing at 08:00 with 401 errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack token issue\nopenstack --os-cloud ci user show ci-deployer -c enabled -c password_expires_at\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack token issue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">The request you have made requires authentication. (HTTP 401)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack --os-cloud ci user show ci-deployer -c enabled -c password_expires_at\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+---------------------+---------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Field               | Value               |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+---------------------+---------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| enabled             | True                |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| password_expires_at | 2026-11-03T00:00:00 |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+---------------------+---------------------+\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A password policy set 90-day expiry on all users, including the service account used by the pipeline. It had expired at midnight. The team moved the pipeline to an application credential, which they rotate on a schedule they choose, and exempted service users from password expiry rules. Automation should not share a person&#39;s login lifecycle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the admin account for everyday work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the \u003ccode>admin\u003c/code> role to make an error go away\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing one user between pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that names are per domain\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-model","text":"The model"},{"id":"tokens-and-the-catalog","text":"Tokens and the catalog"},{"id":"quotas","text":"Quotas"},{"id":"reading-authentication-errors","text":"Reading authentication errors"},{"id":"incident-everything-worked-yesterday","text":"Incident: Everything worked yesterday"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack: Keystone documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/keystone/latest/"},{"t":"OpenStack: Quotas","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/admin/quotas.html"},{"t":"OpenStack: Application credentials","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/keystone/latest/user/application_credentials.html"}],"text":"Before anything is created in OpenStack, someone has to prove who they are and say which project the work belongs to. Keystone handles that. What you will learn Domains, projects, users and roles Tokens, the service catalog and application credentials Quotas and how a project hits them Reading common authentication errors The model A domain groups users and projects, which lets one cloud serve several organisations. A project (older name: tenant) owns resources such as servers and networks, and quotas are applied to it. A user is a person or a service account. A role is a name such as admin or member . A user gets a role on a project . The same user can be admin on one project and a plain member on another. Permissions are decided by policy files in each service, which map roles to actions. By default, members can manage resources in their project, and admin can manage the whole cloud. Tokens and the catalog You sign in and receive a token (a temporary credential), which you present to every service. The token is scoped to one project. The service catalog returned with it lists the API endpoints, so the client knows where Nova and Neutron live. $ env | grep ^OS_ OS_AUTH_URL=https://cloud.example.com:5000/v3 OS_PROJECT_NAME=shop OS_USERNAME=asha OS_USER_DOMAIN_NAME=Default OS_PROJECT_DOMAIN_NAME=Default $ openstack catalog list -c Name -c Type +----------+----------+ | Name | Type | +----------+----------+ | keystone | identity | | nova | compute | | neutron | network | +----------+----------+ For automation, prefer application credentials : a secret limited to one project and, if you choose, to certain roles, that can be revoked without changing a person&#39;s password. Quotas Each project has limits on instances, vCPUs, RAM, volumes, floating IPs, security groups and more. They stop one team from using the whole cloud. $ openstack quota show -c cores -c instances -c ram +-----------+-------+ | Resource | Limit | +-----------+-------+ | cores | 20 | | instances | 10 | | ram | 51200 | +-----------+-------+ $ openstack server create --flavor m1.xlarge --image ubuntu app-9 Quota exceeded for cores: Requested 8, but already used 16 of 20 cores (HTTP 403) A quota error is not a fault in the cloud: it is a rule. The remedies are to delete unused resources, use a smaller flavor, or ask an admin to raise the quota. Raising quotas for everyone to avoid the question removes the protection. Reading authentication errors HTTP 401 Unauthorized : the token or password is wrong or expired. Check the OS_ variables and the domain names. A password right for one domain fails in another. HTTP 403 Forbidden : you are authenticated but the policy denies the action for your role. Ask for the right role on the project. Could not find project : the project name is wrong, or the project is in a different domain. Clock skew : tokens carry timestamps. A server whose clock is far off will reject valid tokens. Keep NTP running on every node. Incident: Everything worked yesterday Incident A deployment script began failing at 08:00 with 401 errors. $ openstack token issue The request you have made requires authentication. (HTTP 401) $ openstack --os-cloud ci user show ci-deployer -c enabled -c password_expires_at +---------------------+---------------------+ | Field | Value | +---------------------+---------------------+ | enabled | True | | password_expires_at | 2026-11-03T00:00:00 | +---------------------+---------------------+ A password policy set 90-day expiry on all users, including the service account used by the pipeline. It had expired at midnight. The team moved the pipeline to an application credential, which they rotate on a schedule they choose, and exempted service users from password expiry rules. Automation should not share a person&#39;s login lifecycle. Common mistakes Using the admin account for everyday work Giving the admin role to make an error go away Sharing one user between pipelines Forgetting that names are per domain"},"openstack/compute-flavors-images-and-scheduling":{"html":"\u003cp>Booting a server looks simple from the outside: pick a flavor and an image, press create. Inside, Nova has to find a compute node with enough room and then build the machine there. When it cannot, the server ends in ERROR.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Flavors, images and the boot sequence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How the scheduler and Placement choose a host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Server states and the fault field\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overcommit ratios and host aggregates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"flavors-and-images\">Flavors and images\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>flavor\u003c/strong> defines size: vCPUs, RAM and root disk. An \u003cstrong>image\u003c/strong> (from Glance) is the starting disk. A server is a flavor plus an image plus a network. Cloud images use \u003cstrong>cloud-init\u003c/strong> to set up users and keys on first boot, using metadata that Nova provides.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack flavor list -c Name -c RAM -c VCPUs -c Disk\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack flavor list -c Name -c RAM -c VCPUs -c Disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+-------+-------+------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Name     |   RAM | VCPUs | Disk |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+-------+-------+------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| m1.small |  2048 |     1 |   20 |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| m1.large |  8192 |     4 |   80 |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| m1.xlarge| 16384 |     8 |  160 |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+-------+-------+------+\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-boot-sequence\">The boot sequence\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>The API checks the request and your quota, and stores the server in the database.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>scheduler\u003c/strong> asks \u003cstrong>Placement\u003c/strong> which compute nodes have enough free resources, then applies filters (for example, the right availability zone or aggregate) and weighs the candidates.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The chosen compute node downloads the image, asks Neutron for a port and Cinder for volumes if needed, and starts the guest through libvirt.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The state moves from \u003ccode>BUILD\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>ACTIVE\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Any step can fail. The server then shows \u003ccode>ERROR\u003c/code> and a \u003cstrong>fault\u003c/strong> message.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack server show app-9 -c status -c fault\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server show app-9 -c status -c fault\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+--------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Field  | Value                                                                   |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+--------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| status | ERROR                                                                   |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| fault  | {'code': 500, 'message': 'No valid host was found. There are not enough hosts available.'} |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+--------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"no-valid-host-was-found\">&quot;No valid host was found&quot;\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>This is the scheduler saying that no compute node passed every check. It does not say which check failed. Work through the common reasons:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Not enough free RAM, vCPU or disk\u003c/strong> on any one node for this flavor. A flavor larger than the biggest free slot cannot be placed even if the total free across the cloud is large.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>The node is disabled or down.\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>openstack compute service list\u003c/code> shows each nova-compute as \u003ccode>up\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>down\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Wrong availability zone or aggregate\u003c/strong>, or a flavor that requires special hardware properties that no node has.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Affinity rules\u003c/strong> that cannot be satisfied.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Volume or network placement\u003c/strong> that limits where the server can go.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack compute service list --service nova-compute -c Host -c Status -c State\nopenstack hypervisor stats show -c memory_mb_used -c memory_mb\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service list --service nova-compute -c Host -c Status -c State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+---------+---------+-------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Host    | Status  | State |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+---------+---------+-------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| cmp-1   | enabled | up    |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| cmp-2   | enabled | down  |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| cmp-3   | disabled| up    |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+---------+---------+-------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack hypervisor stats show -c memory_mb_used -c memory_mb\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The scheduler log (\u003ccode>nova-scheduler.log\u003c/code> on the controller) lists how many hosts each filter rejected. Reading it turns a vague error into a specific one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"overcommit\">Overcommit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default OpenStack allows more vCPUs than physical cores (the allocation ratio for CPU is 16 to 1) and some RAM overcommit (the default ratio is 1.5 to 1 in current releases). Operators change these ratios per cluster. Aggregates group hosts with different settings, such as a &quot;gold&quot; group at 1 to 1 for databases.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-big-flavor-and-a-half-empty-cloud\">Incident: A big flavor and a half-empty cloud\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team could not boot a 16 GB server even though the cloud showed 200 GB free.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack server create --flavor m1.xlarge --image ubuntu db-1\nopenstack server show db-1 -c status -c fault -f value\nopenstack resource provider usage show cmp-1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server create --flavor m1.xlarge --image ubuntu db-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server show db-1 -c status -c fault -f value\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{'code': 500, 'message': 'No valid host was found. There are not enough hosts available.'}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack resource provider usage show cmp-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">MEMORY_MB used 61440 total 65536\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Total free memory was spread as small gaps over many compute nodes: each node had under 8 GB free, so none could hold a 16 GB server. The cloud was fragmented, not full. The operators moved some servers to open up room on one node and, for the long term, reserved a set of large hosts in an aggregate for large flavors. Free space has to be free on one node.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> No valid host found for a large flavor, so delete the error server and retry in a loop.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>while true; do openstack server create --flavor m1.xlarge --image ubuntu app; sleep 5; done\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>Server in ERROR: No valid host was found\n# 200 failed servers pile up and consume quota\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> The scheduler failed because no hypervisor had enough free memory in one piece. Retrying does not change capacity, and each failure uses quota and creates noise.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>openstack hypervisor stats show\nopenstack server show app -f value -c fault\nopenstack flavor list\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Compare the flavor with free resources per host, then use a smaller flavor, free capacity or add a node.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading &quot;No valid host&quot; as meaning the cloud is full\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not checking whether compute services are up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing flavors that live servers use\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving failed ERROR servers around: they still count against quota\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"flavors-and-images","text":"Flavors and images"},{"id":"the-boot-sequence","text":"The boot sequence"},{"id":"no-valid-host-was-found","text":"No valid host was found"},{"id":"overcommit","text":"Overcommit"},{"id":"incident-a-big-flavor-and-a-half-empty-cloud","text":"Incident: A big flavor and a half-empty cloud"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack: Nova documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/"},{"t":"OpenStack: Compute scheduler","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/admin/scheduling.html"},{"t":"OpenStack: Placement","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/placement/latest/"}],"text":"Booting a server looks simple from the outside: pick a flavor and an image, press create. Inside, Nova has to find a compute node with enough room and then build the machine there. When it cannot, the server ends in ERROR. What you will learn Flavors, images and the boot sequence How the scheduler and Placement choose a host Server states and the fault field Overcommit ratios and host aggregates Flavors and images A flavor defines size: vCPUs, RAM and root disk. An image (from Glance) is the starting disk. A server is a flavor plus an image plus a network. Cloud images use cloud-init to set up users and keys on first boot, using metadata that Nova provides. $ openstack flavor list -c Name -c RAM -c VCPUs -c Disk +----------+-------+-------+------+ | Name | RAM | VCPUs | Disk | +----------+-------+-------+------+ | m1.small | 2048 | 1 | 20 | | m1.large | 8192 | 4 | 80 | | m1.xlarge| 16384 | 8 | 160 | +----------+-------+-------+------+ The boot sequence The API checks the request and your quota, and stores the server in the database. The scheduler asks Placement which compute nodes have enough free resources, then applies filters (for example, the right availability zone or aggregate) and weighs the candidates. The chosen compute node downloads the image, asks Neutron for a port and Cinder for volumes if needed, and starts the guest through libvirt. The state moves from BUILD to ACTIVE . Any step can fail. The server then shows ERROR and a fault message. $ openstack server show app-9 -c status -c fault +--------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | Field | Value | +--------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | status | ERROR | | fault | {'code': 500, 'message': 'No valid host was found. There are not enough hosts available.'} | +--------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+ \"No valid host was found\" This is the scheduler saying that no compute node passed every check. It does not say which check failed. Work through the common reasons: Not enough free RAM, vCPU or disk on any one node for this flavor. A flavor larger than the biggest free slot cannot be placed even if the total free across the cloud is large. The node is disabled or down. openstack compute service list shows each nova-compute as up or down . Wrong availability zone or aggregate , or a flavor that requires special hardware properties that no node has. Affinity rules that cannot be satisfied. Volume or network placement that limits where the server can go. $ openstack compute service list --service nova-compute -c Host -c Status -c State +---------+---------+-------+ | Host | Status | State | +---------+---------+-------+ | cmp-1 | enabled | up | | cmp-2 | enabled | down | | cmp-3 | disabled| up | +---------+---------+-------+ $ openstack hypervisor stats show -c memory_mb_used -c memory_mb The scheduler log ( nova-scheduler.log on the controller) lists how many hosts each filter rejected. Reading it turns a vague error into a specific one. Overcommit By default OpenStack allows more vCPUs than physical cores (the allocation ratio for CPU is 16 to 1) and some RAM overcommit (the default ratio is 1.5 to 1 in current releases). Operators change these ratios per cluster. Aggregates group hosts with different settings, such as a \"gold\" group at 1 to 1 for databases. Incident: A big flavor and a half-empty cloud Incident A team could not boot a 16 GB server even though the cloud showed 200 GB free. $ openstack server create --flavor m1.xlarge --image ubuntu db-1 $ openstack server show db-1 -c status -c fault -f value ERROR {'code': 500, 'message': 'No valid host was found. There are not enough hosts available.'} $ openstack resource provider usage show cmp-1 MEMORY_MB used 61440 total 65536 Total free memory was spread as small gaps over many compute nodes: each node had under 8 GB free, so none could hold a 16 GB server. The cloud was fragmented, not"},"openstack/neutron-networking-security-groups-and-floating-ips":{"html":"\u003cp>Neutron gives each project its own virtual networks, routers and firewalls. Most problems reported as &quot;the server is broken&quot; turn out to be network problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Networks, subnets, ports and routers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Provider and self-service networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security groups and floating IPs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A checklist for &quot;I cannot reach my server&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-building-blocks\">The building blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>network\u003c/strong> is a virtual layer-2 segment, and a \u003cstrong>subnet\u003c/strong> is an address range on it, with DHCP.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>port\u003c/strong> is a virtual network card. A server gets one port per interface.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>router\u003c/strong> connects subnets and joins a private network to an external one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Provider networks\u003c/strong> map directly to a physical network, such as a VLAN, and are managed by the operators. \u003cstrong>Self-service networks\u003c/strong> (tenant networks) are created by projects, and use overlays (VXLAN or Geneve) so that many projects can use the same address ranges without clashing.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The implementation behind it is Open vSwitch or OVN on each node, plus agents for DHCP, metadata and routing.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack network list -c Name -c Subnets\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack network list -c Name -c Subnets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+--------------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Name     | Subnets                              |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+--------------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| public   | 5a0c... (203.0.113.0/24)             |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| private  | 7d21... (10.0.0.0/24)                |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+----------+--------------------------------------+\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security-groups\">Security groups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>security group\u003c/strong> is a set of allow rules applied to a server&#39;s port. The default group allows all outbound traffic and denies inbound traffic from outside, except from members of the same group. There are no deny rules: if nothing allows it, it is dropped. A port can have several groups, and their rules add up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack security group rule list web --long -c &quot;IP Protocol&quot; -c &quot;Port Range&quot; -c &quot;IP Range&quot; -c Direction\nopenstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 22 --remote-ip 203.0.113.7/32 web\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack security group rule list web --long -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;IP Protocol&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Port Range&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;IP Range&quot;\u003c/span> -c Direction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+-------------+------------+-----------+-----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| IP Protocol | Port Range | IP Range  | Direction |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+-------------+------------+-----------+-----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| tcp         | 80:80      | 0.0.0.0/0 | ingress   |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+-------------+------------+-----------+-----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 22 --remote-ip 203.0.113.7/32 web\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Opening a port to \u003ccode>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/code> exposes it to the whole internet. Limit management ports such as SSH to known addresses or a jump host.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"floating-ips\">Floating IPs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Servers on private networks have private addresses. A \u003cstrong>floating IP\u003c/strong> is a public address that Neutron maps to a server&#39;s private address using NAT at the router. It can be moved to another server, which is useful for failover.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack floating ip create public -c floating_ip_address -f value\nopenstack server add floating ip web-1 203.0.113.45\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack floating ip create public -c floating_ip_address -f value\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">203.0.113.45\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server add floating ip web-1 203.0.113.45\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"i-cannot-reach-my-server\">&quot;I cannot reach my server&quot;\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check from the inside out:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is the server \u003ccode>ACTIVE\u003c/code>, and did it boot? \u003ccode>openstack console log show web-1\u003c/code> shows the boot log and whether it got an address from DHCP.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does the port have the right fixed IP? \u003ccode>openstack port list --server web-1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does the security group allow your traffic from your address, on the right port and protocol?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is there a floating IP, and is the router attached to the external network?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the service listening inside the server? Console or another server on the same network can test.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Does the server&#39;s own firewall (iptables, ufw) allow it?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>A frequent cause is steps 3 and 4: either the rule is missing or the floating IP is not attached.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ssh-works-from-the-office-but-not-from-home\">Incident: SSH works from the office but not from home\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer working from home could not SSH to a server that colleagues in the office reached fine.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack security group rule list bastion --long -c &quot;Port Range&quot; -c &quot;IP Range&quot; -c Direction\ncurl -s ifconfig.me\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack security group rule list bastion --long -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Port Range&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;IP Range&quot;\u003c/span> -c Direction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+------------+------------------+-----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Port Range | IP Range         | Direction |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+------------+------------------+-----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| 22:22      | 198.51.100.0/24  | ingress   |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+------------+------------------+-----------+\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s ifconfig.me\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">203.0.113.88\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The security group allowed SSH only from the office network, 198.51.100.0/24. The engineer&#39;s home address, 203.0.113.88, was outside it. The fix was to add a rule for that single address with a /32, not to open SSH to the world, and later to move access behind a VPN. The cloud was behaving exactly as configured.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> SSH to a new server times out; add a rule that allows everything.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>openstack security group rule create --protocol any --remote-ip 0.0.0.0/0 default\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(SSH works)\n# later\nscan from the internet finds open 3306 and 6379\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> The default group applies to every server that uses it, so a wide rule exposes databases and caches too. The fault was simply a missing rule for port 22 from a known range.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>openstack security group rule create --protocol tcp --dst-port 22 --remote-ip 203.0.113.0/24 bastion\nopenstack server add security group app bastion\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Open only the needed port from a known source, in a group made for the purpose.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Opening 22 or 3389 to 0.0.0.0/0 to make a problem go away\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that security groups only allow, and default denies inbound\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a floating IP exists because the server has a private address\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing the router when the security group is the cause\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-building-blocks","text":"The building blocks"},{"id":"security-groups","text":"Security groups"},{"id":"floating-ips","text":"Floating IPs"},{"id":"i-cannot-reach-my-server","text":"I cannot reach my server"},{"id":"incident-ssh-works-from-the-office-but-not-from-home","text":"Incident: SSH works from the office but not from home"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack: Neutron documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/neutron/latest/"},{"t":"OpenStack: Security groups","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/neutron/latest/admin/intro-os-networking.html"},{"t":"OpenStack: Networking guide","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/neutron/latest/admin/index.html"}],"text":"Neutron gives each project its own virtual networks, routers and firewalls. Most problems reported as \"the server is broken\" turn out to be network problems. What you will learn Networks, subnets, ports and routers Provider and self-service networks Security groups and floating IPs A checklist for \"I cannot reach my server\" The building blocks A network is a virtual layer-2 segment, and a subnet is an address range on it, with DHCP. A port is a virtual network card. A server gets one port per interface. A router connects subnets and joins a private network to an external one. Provider networks map directly to a physical network, such as a VLAN, and are managed by the operators. Self-service networks (tenant networks) are created by projects, and use overlays (VXLAN or Geneve) so that many projects can use the same address ranges without clashing. The implementation behind it is Open vSwitch or OVN on each node, plus agents for DHCP, metadata and routing. $ openstack network list -c Name -c Subnets +----------+--------------------------------------+ | Name | Subnets | +----------+--------------------------------------+ | public | 5a0c... (203.0.113.0/24) | | private | 7d21... (10.0.0.0/24) | +----------+--------------------------------------+ Security groups A security group is a set of allow rules applied to a server&#39;s port. The default group allows all outbound traffic and denies inbound traffic from outside, except from members of the same group. There are no deny rules: if nothing allows it, it is dropped. A port can have several groups, and their rules add up. $ openstack security group rule list web --long -c \"IP Protocol\" -c \"Port Range\" -c \"IP Range\" -c Direction +-------------+------------+-----------+-----------+ | IP Protocol | Port Range | IP Range | Direction | +-------------+------------+-----------+-----------+ | tcp | 80:80 | 0.0.0.0/0 | ingress | +-------------+------------+-----------+-----------+ $ openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 22 --remote-ip 203.0.113.7/32 web Opening a port to 0.0.0.0/0 exposes it to the whole internet. Limit management ports such as SSH to known addresses or a jump host. Floating IPs Servers on private networks have private addresses. A floating IP is a public address that Neutron maps to a server&#39;s private address using NAT at the router. It can be moved to another server, which is useful for failover. $ openstack floating ip create public -c floating_ip_address -f value 203.0.113.45 $ openstack server add floating ip web-1 203.0.113.45 \"I cannot reach my server\" Check from the inside out: Is the server ACTIVE , and did it boot? openstack console log show web-1 shows the boot log and whether it got an address from DHCP. Does the port have the right fixed IP? openstack port list --server web-1 . Does the security group allow your traffic from your address, on the right port and protocol? Is there a floating IP, and is the router attached to the external network? Is the service listening inside the server? Console or another server on the same network can test. Does the server&#39;s own firewall (iptables, ufw) allow it? A frequent cause is steps 3 and 4: either the rule is missing or the floating IP is not attached. Incident: SSH works from the office but not from home Incident An engineer working from home could not SSH to a server that colleagues in the office reached fine. $ openstack security group rule list bastion --long -c \"Port Range\" -c \"IP Range\" -c Direction +------------+------------------+-----------+ | Port Range | IP Range | Direction | +------------+------------------+-----------+ | 22:22 | 198.51.100.0/24 | ingress | +------------+------------------+-----------+ $ curl -s ifconfig.me 203.0.113.88 The security group allowed SSH only from the office network, 198.51.100.0/24. The engineer&#39;s home address, 203.0.113.88, was outside it. The fix was to add a rule for that single address with a /32, not to open SSH to the world, and later "},"openstack/operating-openstack-logs-upgrades-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>Running a private cloud is an operations job. Most days it is routine, and the skill is knowing where to look when it is not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where each service logs and how to follow a request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stuck states and safe ways to reset them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks for the shared dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrades, backups and capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"following-a-request\">Following a request\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every API request has a \u003cstrong>request id\u003c/strong> that appears in the logs of each service it touches. When a user reports a failed server creation, get the request id and search for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack --debug server create --flavor m1.small --image ubuntu t1 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -i &quot;x-openstack-request-id&quot; | head -1\nsudo grep req-6b9f2a5c /var/log/nova/*.log | cut -c1-140\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack --debug server create --flavor m1.small --image ubuntu t1 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;x-openstack-request-id&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">x-openstack-request-id: req-6b9f2a5c-1d1e-4a63-a1f0-2d9a8cb7e411\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> grep req-6b9f2a5c /var/log/nova/*.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cut\u003c/span> -c1-140\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nova-api.log:  req-6b9f... POST /v2.1/servers  status: 202\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nova-conductor.log: req-6b9f... Failed to schedule instances: NoValidHost_Remote\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nova-scheduler.log: req-6b9f... Filter ComputeFilter returned 0 hosts\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The trail leads from the API to the scheduler, which names the filter that rejected every host. Log paths vary by deployment: \u003ccode>/var/log/nova/\u003c/code> on package installs, \u003ccode>/var/log/kolla/nova/\u003c/code> or container logs for Kolla-based ones. Each service has its own: nova-api, nova-scheduler, nova-conductor, nova-compute, neutron-server, cinder-volume and so on.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-of-the-shared-parts\">Health of the shared parts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack compute service list -c Binary -c Host -c State\nopenstack network agent list -c &quot;Agent Type&quot; -c Host -c Alive\nopenstack volume service list -c Binary -c Host -c State\nsudo rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages | sort -k2 -n -r | head -3\nmysql -e &quot;SHOW STATUS LIKE 'wsrep_cluster_size';&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service list -c Binary -c Host -c State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack network agent list -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Agent Type&quot;\u003c/span> -c Host -c Alive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack volume service list -c Binary -c Host -c State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -k2 -n -r | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mysql -e \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SHOW STATUS LIKE &#x27;wsrep_cluster_size&#x27;;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A down agent means that host&#39;s part of the service is gone. A queue with thousands of waiting messages means consumers are not keeping up or have stopped. A Galera database that has lost members may have lost quorum and refuse writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stuck-states\">Stuck states\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Servers can stay in \u003ccode>BUILD\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>DELETING\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>ERROR\u003c/code>. Volumes can stay in \u003ccode>attaching\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>error_deleting\u003c/code>. Usually the cause is that a message was lost or an agent was down at the time. Steps:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Read the fault and the logs: do not reset blindly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check that the target compute or storage service is healthy now.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For servers, \u003ccode>openstack server set --state active &lt;id&gt;\u003c/code> (admin) corrects the database record, but does not start anything. For volumes, \u003ccode>openstack volume set --state available &lt;id&gt;\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Then retry the real operation, and confirm in the hypervisor (\u003ccode>virsh list\u003c/code>) that the truth matches the record.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Resetting state only edits the database. If the guest is really running or the volume is really attached, a wrong reset creates a second copy or corrupts data. Compare reality first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrades-and-backups\">Upgrades and backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>OpenStack releases about every six months. Upgrading means moving service by service, with database migrations, usually from one release to the next (some releases support skipping). Practise in a test cloud with a copy of the real data. Always:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Back up the databases and configuration before every step, and prove a restore works.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read the release notes for removed settings.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrade the control plane before compute nodes, which can run older agents for a short time.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep the plan reversible where possible.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity\">Capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Watch the ratio of used to allocatable vCPU, RAM and storage, with headroom to lose a compute node and still re-run its servers. Track the number of ERROR servers, failed API requests, queue depth and database size. A cloud that is at 90 percent in one flavor class will fail large requests even when totals look fine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-volume-that-would-not-detach\">Incident: The volume that would not detach\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A volume stuck in \u003ccode>detaching\u003c/code> blocked a database migration.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack volume show data-vol -c status -c attachments\nsudo virsh domblklist instance-0000004a\nopenstack compute service list --service nova-compute -c Host -c State | grep cmp-7\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack volume show data-vol -c status -c attachments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| status      | detaching |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| attachments | [{'server_id': '3f2a', 'device': '/dev/vdb'}] |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> virsh domblklist instance-0000004a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> vdb   /dev/rbd/volumes/volume-91c2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service list --service nova-compute -c Host -c State | grep cmp-7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| cmp-7 | down |\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The compute node cmp-7 was down, so nothing could complete the detach. The operators first confirmed that the server was not running on any other host, so it was safe, then restored cmp-7. After it came up, they retried the detach. Had they only reset the volume to \u003ccode>available\u003c/code> while the guest still had it attached, two writers could have corrupted the data. They now have a runbook that starts with &quot;check the compute service, then compare the hypervisor with the database&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Resetting states without checking the hypervisor\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading without a tested restore of the database\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on queue depth or Galera cluster size\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a three-controller cluster as safe while one node is down and unnoticed\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"following-a-request","text":"Following a request"},{"id":"health-of-the-shared-parts","text":"Health of the shared parts"},{"id":"stuck-states","text":"Stuck states"},{"id":"upgrades-and-backups","text":"Upgrades and backups"},{"id":"capacity","text":"Capacity"},{"id":"incident-the-volume-that-would-not-detach","text":"Incident: The volume that would not detach"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack: Operations guide","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/operations-guide/"},{"t":"OpenStack: Kolla-Ansible documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/kolla-ansible/latest/"},{"t":"OpenStack: Upgrade guides","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/admin/upgrades.html"}],"text":"Running a private cloud is an operations job. Most days it is routine, and the skill is knowing where to look when it is not. What you will learn Where each service logs and how to follow a request Stuck states and safe ways to reset them Health checks for the shared dependencies Upgrades, backups and capacity Following a request Every API request has a request id that appears in the logs of each service it touches. When a user reports a failed server creation, get the request id and search for it. $ openstack --debug server create --flavor m1.small --image ubuntu t1 2>&1 | grep -i \"x-openstack-request-id\" | head -1 x-openstack-request-id: req-6b9f2a5c-1d1e-4a63-a1f0-2d9a8cb7e411 $ sudo grep req-6b9f2a5c /var/log/nova/*. log | cut -c1-140 nova-api.log: req-6b9f... POST /v2.1/servers status: 202 nova-conductor.log: req-6b9f... Failed to schedule instances: NoValidHost_Remote nova-scheduler.log: req-6b9f... Filter ComputeFilter returned 0 hosts The trail leads from the API to the scheduler, which names the filter that rejected every host. Log paths vary by deployment: /var/log/nova/ on package installs, /var/log/kolla/nova/ or container logs for Kolla-based ones. Each service has its own: nova-api, nova-scheduler, nova-conductor, nova-compute, neutron-server, cinder-volume and so on. Health of the shared parts $ openstack compute service list -c Binary -c Host -c State $ openstack network agent list -c \"Agent Type\" -c Host -c Alive $ openstack volume service list -c Binary -c Host -c State $ sudo rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages | sort -k2 -n -r | head -3 $ mysql -e \"SHOW STATUS LIKE &#x27;wsrep_cluster_size&#x27;;\" A down agent means that host&#39;s part of the service is gone. A queue with thousands of waiting messages means consumers are not keeping up or have stopped. A Galera database that has lost members may have lost quorum and refuse writes. Stuck states Servers can stay in BUILD , DELETING or ERROR . Volumes can stay in attaching or error_deleting . Usually the cause is that a message was lost or an agent was down at the time. Steps: Read the fault and the logs: do not reset blindly. Check that the target compute or storage service is healthy now. For servers, openstack server set --state active \u003cid> (admin) corrects the database record, but does not start anything. For volumes, openstack volume set --state available \u003cid> . Then retry the real operation, and confirm in the hypervisor ( virsh list ) that the truth matches the record. Resetting state only edits the database. If the guest is really running or the volume is really attached, a wrong reset creates a second copy or corrupts data. Compare reality first. Upgrades and backups OpenStack releases about every six months. Upgrading means moving service by service, with database migrations, usually from one release to the next (some releases support skipping). Practise in a test cloud with a copy of the real data. Always: Back up the databases and configuration before every step, and prove a restore works. Read the release notes for removed settings. Upgrade the control plane before compute nodes, which can run older agents for a short time. Keep the plan reversible where possible. Capacity Watch the ratio of used to allocatable vCPU, RAM and storage, with headroom to lose a compute node and still re-run its servers. Track the number of ERROR servers, failed API requests, queue depth and database size. A cloud that is at 90 percent in one flavor class will fail large requests even when totals look fine. Incident: The volume that would not detach Incident A volume stuck in detaching blocked a database migration. $ openstack volume show data-vol -c status -c attachments | status | detaching | | attachments | [{'server_id': '3f2a', 'device': '/dev/vdb'}] | $ sudo virsh domblklist instance-0000004a vdb /dev/rbd/volumes/volume-91c2 $ openstack compute service list --service nova-compute -c Host -c State | grep cmp-7 | cmp-7 | down | The compute node cmp-7 was down, s"},"openstack/openstack-ops-no-valid-host-and-scheduling-failures":{"html":"\u003cp>&quot;No valid host was found&quot; is the most common OpenStack build error, and it says almost nothing by itself. It means that no compute node passed every filter for this request. The skill is to find which filter rejected the hosts and why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The path from boot request to compute node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Placement and the scheduler pick hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading the real reason in the logs and instance faults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Typical causes: capacity, flavor, aggregates, availability zones and cells\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-path-of-a-request\">The path of a request\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>nova-api\u003c/code> accepts the request and stores the instance. \u003ccode>nova-conductor\u003c/code> coordinates. The \u003ccode>nova-scheduler\u003c/code> asks \u003cstrong>Placement\u003c/strong> for candidate resource providers that have enough VCPU, memory and disk (and any traits or resources the flavor requires), then applies filters (availability zone, aggregate matching, image properties, anti-affinity) and weighs the survivors. The chosen compute node builds the guest, asking Neutron for ports and Cinder for volumes. A failure at any step sends the instance to \u003ccode>ERROR\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack server show web-01 -c status -c fault\nopenstack server event list web-01\nopenstack flavor show m1.large\nopenstack compute service list\nopenstack hypervisor list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server show web-01 -c status -c fault\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server event list web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack flavor show m1.large\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack hypervisor list\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>fault\u003c/code> field and the event list tell you the request ID, which you can search in the scheduler and conductor logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ask-placement\">Ask Placement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack resource provider list\nopenstack resource provider usage show 7c6a1c2e-1111-4a8b-9ef2-0d7d5d5a0a11\nopenstack resource provider inventory list 7c6a1c2e-1111-4a8b-9ef2-0d7d5d5a0a11\nopenstack allocation candidate list --resource VCPU=8 --resource MEMORY_MB=32768 --resource DISK_GB=100\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack resource provider list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack resource provider usage show 7c6a1c2e-1111-4a8b-9ef2-0d7d5d5a0a11\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack resource provider inventory list 7c6a1c2e-1111-4a8b-9ef2-0d7d5d5a0a11\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack allocation candidate list --resource VCPU=8 --resource MEMORY_MB=32768 --resource DISK_GB=100\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The candidate list answers: for this size, which providers fit? An empty list means a real capacity problem (including the allocation ratios) or a request that asks for something no node offers (a required trait, a very large flavor, PCI devices). If candidates exist but the build still fails, a scheduler filter rejected them: read the scheduler log with the request ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-causes\">Common causes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>No capacity\u003c/strong> at the configured allocation ratios, or hosts disabled or down (\u003ccode>compute service list\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>State: down\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Status: disabled\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Flavor asks for too much\u003c/strong> (extra specs for NUMA, huge pages, a trait) and few hosts match.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Aggregate or AZ mismatch\u003c/strong>: the image or flavor targets an aggregate with no free hosts, or the AZ has none.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Anti-affinity\u003c/strong>: a server group forbids placing on the few remaining hosts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>New hosts not mapped\u003c/strong>: compute services exist, but \u003ccode>nova-manage cell_v2 discover_hosts\u003c/code> was not run, so the cell does not know them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Quota\u003c/strong> errors are different: they are rejected before scheduling, with a message about limits.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> allocation candidate list returns candidates, but the build still says No valid host. Where next?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A scheduler filter rejected the candidates. Search the scheduler log by the request ID for the filter and the count of hosts removed at each step.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Placement says who has room; filters say who is allowed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-plenty-of-room-nowhere-to-put-it\">Incident: Plenty of room, nowhere to put it\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A project could not create instances of its new flavor, while the dashboard showed 40 percent free capacity.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack flavor show m1.numa -c properties\nopenstack allocation candidate list --resource VCPU=8 --resource MEMORY_MB=32768 --resource DISK_GB=50 | wc -l\nopenstack hypervisor list --long | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack flavor show m1.numa -c properties\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">properties | hw:cpu_policy='dedicated', hw:mem_page_size='large'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack allocation candidate list --resource VCPU=8 --resource MEMORY_MB=32768 --resource DISK_GB=50 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack hypervisor list --long | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The flavor needed dedicated cores and huge pages. Only two hosts in a small aggregate were configured for that, and both were full, while the free capacity was on general-purpose hosts. The operations team added hosts to the aggregate with the right configuration, made the dashboard show capacity per aggregate and per flavor class, and documented how special flavors map to host pools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading No valid host as a pure capacity problem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not following the request ID across services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting host discovery after adding compute nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Showing total capacity without breaking it down by flavor class\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-path-of-a-request","text":"The path of a request"},{"id":"ask-placement","text":"Ask Placement"},{"id":"common-causes","text":"Common causes"},{"id":"incident-plenty-of-room-nowhere-to-put-it","text":"Incident: Plenty of room, nowhere to put it"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Nova: scheduling","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/admin/scheduling.html"},{"t":"Placement documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/placement/latest/"},{"t":"Nova administration guide","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/admin/index.html"}],"text":"\"No valid host was found\" is the most common OpenStack build error, and it says almost nothing by itself. It means that no compute node passed every filter for this request. The skill is to find which filter rejected the hosts and why. What you will learn The path from boot request to compute node How Placement and the scheduler pick hosts Reading the real reason in the logs and instance faults Typical causes: capacity, flavor, aggregates, availability zones and cells The path of a request nova-api accepts the request and stores the instance. nova-conductor coordinates. The nova-scheduler asks Placement for candidate resource providers that have enough VCPU, memory and disk (and any traits or resources the flavor requires), then applies filters (availability zone, aggregate matching, image properties, anti-affinity) and weighs the survivors. The chosen compute node builds the guest, asking Neutron for ports and Cinder for volumes. A failure at any step sends the instance to ERROR . $ openstack server show web-01 -c status -c fault $ openstack server event list web-01 $ openstack flavor show m1.large $ openstack compute service list $ openstack hypervisor list The fault field and the event list tell you the request ID, which you can search in the scheduler and conductor logs. Ask Placement $ openstack resource provider list $ openstack resource provider usage show 7c6a1c2e-1111-4a8b-9ef2-0d7d5d5a0a11 $ openstack resource provider inventory list 7c6a1c2e-1111-4a8b-9ef2-0d7d5d5a0a11 $ openstack allocation candidate list --resource VCPU=8 --resource MEMORY_MB=32768 --resource DISK_GB=100 The candidate list answers: for this size, which providers fit? An empty list means a real capacity problem (including the allocation ratios) or a request that asks for something no node offers (a required trait, a very large flavor, PCI devices). If candidates exist but the build still fails, a scheduler filter rejected them: read the scheduler log with the request ID. Common causes No capacity at the configured allocation ratios, or hosts disabled or down ( compute service list shows State: down or Status: disabled ). Flavor asks for too much (extra specs for NUMA, huge pages, a trait) and few hosts match. Aggregate or AZ mismatch : the image or flavor targets an aggregate with no free hosts, or the AZ has none. Anti-affinity : a server group forbids placing on the few remaining hosts. New hosts not mapped : compute services exist, but nova-manage cell_v2 discover_hosts was not run, so the cell does not know them. Quota errors are different: they are rejected before scheduling, with a message about limits. Predict first. allocation candidate list returns candidates, but the build still says No valid host. Where next? Your guess Answer: A scheduler filter rejected the candidates. Search the scheduler log by the request ID for the filter and the count of hosts removed at each step. Placement says who has room; filters say who is allowed. Incident: Plenty of room, nowhere to put it Incident A project could not create instances of its new flavor, while the dashboard showed 40 percent free capacity. $ openstack flavor show m1.numa -c properties properties | hw:cpu_policy='dedicated', hw:mem_page_size='large' $ openstack allocation candidate list --resource VCPU=8 --resource MEMORY_MB=32768 --resource DISK_GB=50 | wc -l 0 $ openstack hypervisor list --long | head -4 The flavor needed dedicated cores and huge pages. Only two hosts in a small aggregate were configured for that, and both were full, while the free capacity was on general-purpose hosts. The operations team added hosts to the aggregate with the right configuration, made the dashboard show capacity per aggregate and per flavor class, and documented how special flavors map to host pools. Common mistakes Reading No valid host as a pure capacity problem Not following the request ID across services Forgetting host discovery after adding compute nodes Showing total capacity without breaking it do"},"openstack/openstack-ops-neutron-and-ceph-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>Two subsystems cause most deep OpenStack incidents: virtual networking, where a packet crosses several software devices, and storage, usually Ceph, where cluster health decides whether volumes respond. Each can be traced step by step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The path of a packet from a guest to the outside\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking agents, ports and flows with the standard tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading \u003ccode>ceph -s\u003c/code> and health details\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixes for slow requests, full OSDs and degraded data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"neutron-follow-the-packet\">Neutron: follow the packet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>First check the control plane: are the agents alive, and is the port bound and active?\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack network agent list\nopenstack port list --server web-01\nopenstack port show 3f1a2b4c-0000-4d5e-9aaa-111122223333 -c status -c binding_vif_type -c binding_host_id\nopenstack floating ip list\nopenstack security group rule list web-sg\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack network agent list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack port list --server web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack port show 3f1a2b4c-0000-4d5e-9aaa-111122223333 -c status -c binding_vif_type -c binding_host_id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack floating ip list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack security group rule list web-sg\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A port with status \u003ccode>DOWN\u003c/code> or a binding failure points to the agent or driver on that host. Missing security group rules are a common cause of &quot;can&#39;t connect&quot; even when everything else is fine. Then trace the data path on the compute host (shown for Open vSwitch):\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo ovs-vsctl show\nsudo ovs-ofctl dump-flows br-int | head\nsudo ip netns list\nsudo ip netns exec qrouter-0a1b2c3d ip addr\nsudo tcpdump -i tap3f1a2b4c-00 -nn icmp\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ovs-vsctl show\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ovs-ofctl dump-flows br-int | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ip netns list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ip netns \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> qrouter-0a1b2c3d ip addr\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> tcpdump -i tap3f1a2b4c-00 -nn icmp\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Capture at each hop: the guest&#39;s tap device, the bridge, the tunnel or VLAN interface, and the router or DHCP namespace on the network node. The first hop where packets appear but then vanish tells you which rule, MTU or link to fix. Tunnels (VXLAN or Geneve) reduce the effective MTU, so large packets failing while small ones work usually means an MTU mismatch between guest, tunnel and physical network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ceph-read-the-health\">Ceph: read the health\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph -s\nceph health detail\nceph osd tree\nceph osd df\nceph pg stat\nceph osd perf\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph -s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph health detail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph osd tree\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph osd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph pg \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph osd perf\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ceph -s\u003c/code> shows overall health, monitor quorum, OSDs up and in, and placement group (PG) states. \u003ccode>HEALTH_OK\u003c/code> is the goal. \u003ccode>HEALTH_WARN\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>degraded\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>misplaced\u003c/code> PGs means data is being recovered; the cluster serves I/O but with less protection and extra load. Watch for:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Slow ops\u003c/strong> and a high \u003ccode>commit_latency\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>osd perf\u003c/code>: a failing disk, a saturated network or a recovery that is competing with client traffic.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Near full OSDs\u003c/strong>: Ceph stops writes when OSDs reach the full ratio. Add capacity, rebalance, or delete data before it happens; never ignore the nearfull warning.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>OSDs down\u003c/strong>: check the host, the disk and the service. A single failed disk is routine; many at once suggests network or power.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Recovery speed can be tuned (\u003ccode>osd_max_backfills\u003c/code> and recovery settings) to balance speed against client impact, which is useful during business hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-the-openstack-side\">From the OpenStack side\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cinder volumes and Nova ephemeral disks backed by RBD are slow or stuck when the Ceph pool is unhealthy. If an instance freezes on I/O, check Ceph health before restarting anything, and use \u003ccode>rbd\u003c/code> to inspect images and locks:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rbd ls volumes | head\nrbd status volumes/volume-0a1b2c3d\nrbd info volumes/volume-0a1b2c3d\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rbd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> volumes | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rbd status volumes/volume-0a1b2c3d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rbd info volumes/volume-0a1b2c3d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Pings between two guests on different hosts work, but large file transfers hang. What do you test?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The path MTU. Overlay tunnels reduce MTU. Test with ping and the do-not-fragment flag, and align the guest, tunnel and physical MTUs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Small works, large hangs: think MTU.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cluster-that-stopped-accepting-writes\">Incident: The cluster that stopped accepting writes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At 14:00 many instances froze, and Cinder operations timed out.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph health detail\nceph osd df | sort -k17 -nr | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph health detail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HEALTH_ERR 3 full osd(s); 1 pool(s) full\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">osd.12 is full at 95%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph osd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -k17 -nr | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">12  hdd  ... 96.20\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Several OSDs on one host had filled up faster than the others because of an uneven placement and a large batch import. At the full ratio Ceph blocks writes to protect data. The operators temporarily raised the full ratio a little, deleted old snapshots to free space, rebalanced with the balancer module, and then added disks. They created alerts at 70 and 80 percent and a capacity forecast, so nearfull would never again be a surprise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Restarting instances before checking Ceph health\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring nearfull warnings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capturing packets at only one point\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting overlay overhead when setting MTUs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"neutron-follow-the-packet","text":"Neutron: follow the packet"},{"id":"ceph-read-the-health","text":"Ceph: read the health"},{"id":"from-the-openstack-side","text":"From the OpenStack side"},{"id":"incident-the-cluster-that-stopped-accepting-writes","text":"Incident: The cluster that stopped accepting writes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Neutron administration guide","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/neutron/latest/admin/index.html"},{"t":"Ceph: health checks","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/health-checks/"},{"t":"Ceph: troubleshooting OSDs","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-osd/"}],"text":"Two subsystems cause most deep OpenStack incidents: virtual networking, where a packet crosses several software devices, and storage, usually Ceph, where cluster health decides whether volumes respond. Each can be traced step by step. What you will learn The path of a packet from a guest to the outside Checking agents, ports and flows with the standard tools Reading ceph -s and health details Fixes for slow requests, full OSDs and degraded data Neutron: follow the packet First check the control plane: are the agents alive, and is the port bound and active? $ openstack network agent list $ openstack port list --server web-01 $ openstack port show 3f1a2b4c-0000-4d5e-9aaa-111122223333 -c status -c binding_vif_type -c binding_host_id $ openstack floating ip list $ openstack security group rule list web-sg A port with status DOWN or a binding failure points to the agent or driver on that host. Missing security group rules are a common cause of \"can&#39;t connect\" even when everything else is fine. Then trace the data path on the compute host (shown for Open vSwitch): $ sudo ovs-vsctl show $ sudo ovs-ofctl dump-flows br-int | head $ sudo ip netns list $ sudo ip netns exec qrouter-0a1b2c3d ip addr $ sudo tcpdump -i tap3f1a2b4c-00 -nn icmp Capture at each hop: the guest&#39;s tap device, the bridge, the tunnel or VLAN interface, and the router or DHCP namespace on the network node. The first hop where packets appear but then vanish tells you which rule, MTU or link to fix. Tunnels (VXLAN or Geneve) reduce the effective MTU, so large packets failing while small ones work usually means an MTU mismatch between guest, tunnel and physical network. Ceph: read the health $ ceph -s $ ceph health detail $ ceph osd tree $ ceph osd df $ ceph pg stat $ ceph osd perf ceph -s shows overall health, monitor quorum, OSDs up and in, and placement group (PG) states. HEALTH_OK is the goal. HEALTH_WARN with degraded or misplaced PGs means data is being recovered; the cluster serves I/O but with less protection and extra load. Watch for: Slow ops and a high commit_latency in osd perf : a failing disk, a saturated network or a recovery that is competing with client traffic. Near full OSDs : Ceph stops writes when OSDs reach the full ratio. Add capacity, rebalance, or delete data before it happens; never ignore the nearfull warning. OSDs down : check the host, the disk and the service. A single failed disk is routine; many at once suggests network or power. Recovery speed can be tuned ( osd_max_backfills and recovery settings) to balance speed against client impact, which is useful during business hours. From the OpenStack side Cinder volumes and Nova ephemeral disks backed by RBD are slow or stuck when the Ceph pool is unhealthy. If an instance freezes on I/O, check Ceph health before restarting anything, and use rbd to inspect images and locks: $ rbd ls volumes | head $ rbd status volumes/volume-0a1b2c3d $ rbd info volumes/volume-0a1b2c3d Predict first. Pings between two guests on different hosts work, but large file transfers hang. What do you test? Your guess Answer: The path MTU. Overlay tunnels reduce MTU. Test with ping and the do-not-fragment flag, and align the guest, tunnel and physical MTUs. Small works, large hangs: think MTU. Incident: The cluster that stopped accepting writes Incident At 14:00 many instances froze, and Cinder operations timed out. $ ceph health detail HEALTH_ERR 3 full osd(s); 1 pool(s) full osd.12 is full at 95% $ ceph osd df | sort -k17 -nr | head -3 12 hdd ... 96.20 Several OSDs on one host had filled up faster than the others because of an uneven placement and a large batch import. At the full ratio Ceph blocks writes to protect data. The operators temporarily raised the full ratio a little, deleted old snapshots to free space, rebalanced with the balancer module, and then added disks. They created alerts at 70 and 80 percent and a capacity forecast, so nearfull would never again be a surprise. Common mistakes Restarting in"},"openstack/openstack-ops-maintenance-evacuation-and-a-day-two-runbook":{"html":"\u003cp>A private cloud has hundreds of moving parts, and hardware fails every week at scale. The difference between a calm operations team and a stressed one is a short set of practiced procedures: drain a host, recover from a dead host, patch without downtime and check health before and after any change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Draining a compute host for planned maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Evacuating instances from a failed host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pre-change and post-change health checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What belongs in a day-two runbook\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"planned-maintenance-drain\">Planned maintenance: drain\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Check that other hosts in the aggregate have capacity (N+1).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stop new placements on the host by disabling its compute service.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Live migrate instances away (or cold migrate those that cannot move live).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do the work, reboot and verify.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enable the service again and watch placement.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack compute service set --disable --disable-reason &quot;kernel patch 2026-03-05&quot; compute-12 nova-compute\nopenstack server list --all-projects --host compute-12 -c ID -c Name -c Status\nopenstack server migrate --live-migration --block-migration 5d2c7e1a-0000-4c11-9d0a-3f4e8a1b2c3d\nopenstack server migration list --status running\nopenstack compute service set --enable compute-12 nova-compute\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">disable\u003c/span> --disable-reason \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kernel patch 2026-03-05&quot;\u003c/span> compute-12 nova-compute\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server list --all-projects --host compute-12 -c ID -c Name -c Status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server migrate --live-migration --block-migration 5d2c7e1a-0000-4c11-9d0a-3f4e8a1b2c3d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server migration list --status running\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> compute-12 nova-compute\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Live migration fails when the destination has an incompatible CPU model, a different network or storage setup, or when the guest changes memory too quickly. Migrate in small batches, check each one finishes, and keep an eye on the network. Instances using local storage need block migration, which is slower. Pinned or SR-IOV guests may require cold migration with a short downtime that you plan with their owners.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"host-failure-evacuate\">Host failure: evacuate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a host dies, instances on it are stuck in their last state. Make sure the host is really down and \u003cstrong>fenced\u003c/strong> (powered off or isolated), because rebuilding an instance elsewhere while the old one still runs against shared storage can corrupt data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack compute service list --service nova-compute\nopenstack server list --host compute-12 --all-projects\nopenstack server evacuate 5d2c7e1a-0000-4c11-9d0a-3f4e8a1b2c3d\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service list --service nova-compute\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server list --host compute-12 --all-projects\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack server evacuate 5d2c7e1a-0000-4c11-9d0a-3f4e8a1b2c3d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Evacuation rebuilds the instance on another host using shared or boot-from-volume storage. Instances with local ephemeral disks lose that data, which is a good reason to prefer boot from volume for anything important. Some deployments automate this with a high-availability service; test it regularly, with clear rules about when it triggers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-checks-around-every-change\">Health checks around every change\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before: all compute services up, all network agents alive, Ceph \u003ccode>HEALTH_OK\u003c/code>, no running migrations, no active alerts, recent backups of the database and configuration. After: the same checks, plus a canary test that creates a server, attaches a volume, assigns a floating IP, pings it and deletes it. Automate the canary and run it continuously.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nopenstack compute service list --service nova-compute -f value -c State | grep -vq down\nopenstack network agent list -f value -c Alive | grep -vq False\nceph health | grep -q HEALTH_OK\necho &quot;preflight ok&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\nopenstack compute service list --service nova-compute -f value -c State | grep -vq down\nopenstack network agent list -f value -c Alive | grep -vq False\nceph health | grep -q HEALTH_OK\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;preflight ok&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-day-two-runbook\">The day-two runbook\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep it short, versioned and tested: contact and escalation list, architecture map, how to drain and evacuate, how to restart each service safely, how to find logs, upgrade steps with rollback points, capacity thresholds and who approves changes. Review after every incident, and link each alert to its runbook section.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A compute host loses power, and you want to evacuate its instances immediately. What must you confirm first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> That the host is really down and fenced, so the old instances cannot still write to shared storage. Then evacuate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Avoid two copies of one instance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-evacuation-that-created-duplicates\">Incident: The evacuation that created duplicates\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A compute host lost its network but kept running, an operator evacuated its instances, and several databases wrote to the same volumes from two hosts.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack compute service list --service nova-compute | grep compute-12\nping -c 2 compute-12-ipmi\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openstack compute service list --service nova-compute | grep compute-12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| compute-12 | nova-compute | nova | enabled | down |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ping -c 2 compute-12-ipmi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2 packets transmitted, 2 received\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The service showed down, but the host was alive and still running its guests. The evacuation started new copies elsewhere while the originals were still running, and two instances held the same volume. The team recovered from backups and wrote a rule: confirm power state through the out-of-band controller and power the host off (fencing) before any evacuation. They later automated that check inside the evacuation tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Evacuating without fencing the failed host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining a host without checking spare capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using local ephemeral disks for important data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the canary test after a change\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"planned-maintenance-drain","text":"Planned maintenance: drain"},{"id":"host-failure-evacuate","text":"Host failure: evacuate"},{"id":"health-checks-around-every-change","text":"Health checks around every change"},{"id":"the-day-two-runbook","text":"The day-two runbook"},{"id":"incident-the-evacuation-that-created-duplicates","text":"Incident: The evacuation that created duplicates"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Nova: evacuate","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/admin/evacuate.html"},{"t":"Nova: live migration usage","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/nova/latest/admin/live-migration-usage.html"},{"t":"OpenStack operations guide","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/operations-guide/"}],"text":"A private cloud has hundreds of moving parts, and hardware fails every week at scale. The difference between a calm operations team and a stressed one is a short set of practiced procedures: drain a host, recover from a dead host, patch without downtime and check health before and after any change. What you will learn Draining a compute host for planned maintenance Evacuating instances from a failed host Pre-change and post-change health checks What belongs in a day-two runbook Planned maintenance: drain Check that other hosts in the aggregate have capacity (N+1). Stop new placements on the host by disabling its compute service. Live migrate instances away (or cold migrate those that cannot move live). Do the work, reboot and verify. Enable the service again and watch placement. $ openstack compute service set -- disable --disable-reason \"kernel patch 2026-03-05\" compute-12 nova-compute $ openstack server list --all-projects --host compute-12 -c ID -c Name -c Status $ openstack server migrate --live-migration --block-migration 5d2c7e1a-0000-4c11-9d0a-3f4e8a1b2c3d $ openstack server migration list --status running $ openstack compute service set -- enable compute-12 nova-compute Live migration fails when the destination has an incompatible CPU model, a different network or storage setup, or when the guest changes memory too quickly. Migrate in small batches, check each one finishes, and keep an eye on the network. Instances using local storage need block migration, which is slower. Pinned or SR-IOV guests may require cold migration with a short downtime that you plan with their owners. Host failure: evacuate If a host dies, instances on it are stuck in their last state. Make sure the host is really down and fenced (powered off or isolated), because rebuilding an instance elsewhere while the old one still runs against shared storage can corrupt data. $ openstack compute service list --service nova-compute $ openstack server list --host compute-12 --all-projects $ openstack server evacuate 5d2c7e1a-0000-4c11-9d0a-3f4e8a1b2c3d Evacuation rebuilds the instance on another host using shared or boot-from-volume storage. Instances with local ephemeral disks lose that data, which is a good reason to prefer boot from volume for anything important. Some deployments automate this with a high-availability service; test it regularly, with clear rules about when it triggers. Health checks around every change Before: all compute services up, all network agents alive, Ceph HEALTH_OK , no running migrations, no active alerts, recent backups of the database and configuration. After: the same checks, plus a canary test that creates a server, attaches a volume, assigns a floating IP, pings it and deletes it. Automate the canary and run it continuously. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail openstack compute service list --service nova-compute -f value -c State | grep -vq down openstack network agent list -f value -c Alive | grep -vq False ceph health | grep -q HEALTH_OK echo \"preflight ok\" The day-two runbook Keep it short, versioned and tested: contact and escalation list, architecture map, how to drain and evacuate, how to restart each service safely, how to find logs, upgrade steps with rollback points, capacity thresholds and who approves changes. Review after every incident, and link each alert to its runbook section. Predict first. A compute host loses power, and you want to evacuate its instances immediately. What must you confirm first? Your guess Answer: That the host is really down and fenced, so the old instances cannot still write to shared storage. Then evacuate. Avoid two copies of one instance. Incident: The evacuation that created duplicates Incident A compute host lost its network but kept running, an operator evacuated its instances, and several databases wrote to the same volumes from two hosts. $ openstack compute service list --service nova-compute | grep compute-12 | compute-12 | nova-compute | nova | enabled | down | $ ping -"},"openstack/cinder-glance-and-storage-services":{"html":"\u003cp>Compute gets the attention, but most day-to-day OpenStack problems involve storage: volumes that will not attach, images that are slow to boot and snapshots that fill pools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cinder volumes, types, snapshots and backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Glance images, properties and formats\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Ceph connects both services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Troubleshooting attach and boot problems\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cinder\">Cinder\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack volume type list\nopenstack volume create --size 100 --type ssd db-data\nopenstack server add volume db-1 db-data\nopenstack volume snapshot create --volume db-data db-data-before-upgrade\nopenstack volume backup create --name db-backup-1 db-data\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openstack volume \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> list\nopenstack volume create --size 100 --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> ssd db-data\nopenstack server add volume db-1 db-data\nopenstack volume snapshot create --volume db-data db-data-before-upgrade\nopenstack volume backup create --name db-backup-1 db-data\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>volume type\u003c/strong> maps to a backend and its settings, such as performance tier. A \u003cstrong>snapshot\u003c/strong> is a point-in-time copy on the same backend and is not a backup. A \u003cstrong>backup\u003c/strong> is stored in a separate service such as object storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"glance\">Glance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack image create --file jammy.qcow2 --disk-format qcow2 --container-format bare \\\n  --property hw_scsi_model=virtio-scsi --property os_type=linux --public jammy-2026-11\nopenstack image list --status active\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openstack image create --file jammy.qcow2 --disk-format qcow2 --container-format bare \\\n  --property hw_scsi_model=virtio-scsi --property os_type=linux --public jammy-2026-11\nopenstack image list --status active\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Image \u003cstrong>properties\u003c/strong> control hardware and scheduling, such as disk bus and required features. With Ceph, store images as \u003cstrong>raw\u003c/strong> so they can be cloned without conversion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"with-ceph\">With Ceph\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When Glance, Cinder and Nova all use Ceph RBD, creating a volume from an image is a copy-on-write clone in the same cluster, and instances can boot from volume without copying data over the network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack volume show db-data -c status -c attachments\ncinder-manage volume list 2&gt;/dev/null | head\ngrep -i error /var/log/cinder/cinder-volume.log | tail\nrbd -p volumes ls | head\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openstack volume show db-data -c status -c attachments\ncinder-manage volume list 2&gt;/dev/null | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\ngrep -i error /var/log/cinder/cinder-volume.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>\nrbd -p volumes \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Typical states: \u003cstrong>error_attaching\u003c/strong> (compute or iSCSI or RBD access problem), \u003cstrong>stuck in deleting\u003c/strong> (a snapshot still exists), \u003cstrong>creating\u003c/strong> for a long time (backend full or the scheduler found no valid backend). Check quotas, backend capacity and the volume service logs, and reset states only after confirming the real state on the backend.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-volume-stuck-in-error-attaching\">Incident: Volume stuck in error_attaching\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database volume would not attach after a compute host replacement.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">status: error_attaching\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">libvirt: failed to open rbd volumes/volume-9c1f: Permission denied\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ceph client.nova lacks access to pool volumes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new host had a different Ceph key that lacked permission on the volumes pool. Once the capabilities were corrected and the secret defined on the host, the attach worked. The team added the Ceph credentials to the host build checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating snapshots as backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resetting volume states without checking the backend\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Converting images on every boot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring pool capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cinder","text":"Cinder"},{"id":"glance","text":"Glance"},{"id":"with-ceph","text":"With Ceph"},{"id":"troubleshooting","text":"Troubleshooting"},{"id":"incident-volume-stuck-in-error-attaching","text":"Incident: Volume stuck in error_attaching"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack Cinder documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/cinder/latest/"},{"t":"OpenStack Glance documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/glance/latest/"}],"text":"Compute gets the attention, but most day-to-day OpenStack problems involve storage: volumes that will not attach, images that are slow to boot and snapshots that fill pools. What you will learn Cinder volumes, types, snapshots and backups Glance images, properties and formats How Ceph connects both services Troubleshooting attach and boot problems Cinder openstack volume type list openstack volume create --size 100 -- type ssd db-data openstack server add volume db-1 db-data openstack volume snapshot create --volume db-data db-data-before-upgrade openstack volume backup create --name db-backup-1 db-data A volume type maps to a backend and its settings, such as performance tier. A snapshot is a point-in-time copy on the same backend and is not a backup. A backup is stored in a separate service such as object storage. Glance openstack image create --file jammy.qcow2 --disk-format qcow2 --container-format bare \\ --property hw_scsi_model=virtio-scsi --property os_type=linux --public jammy-2026-11 openstack image list --status active Image properties control hardware and scheduling, such as disk bus and required features. With Ceph, store images as raw so they can be cloned without conversion. With Ceph When Glance, Cinder and Nova all use Ceph RBD, creating a volume from an image is a copy-on-write clone in the same cluster, and instances can boot from volume without copying data over the network. Troubleshooting openstack volume show db-data -c status -c attachments cinder-manage volume list 2>/dev/null | head grep -i error /var/log/cinder/cinder-volume.log | tail rbd -p volumes ls | head Typical states: error_attaching (compute or iSCSI or RBD access problem), stuck in deleting (a snapshot still exists), creating for a long time (backend full or the scheduler found no valid backend). Check quotas, backend capacity and the volume service logs, and reset states only after confirming the real state on the backend. Incident: Volume stuck in error_attaching Incident A database volume would not attach after a compute host replacement. status: error_attaching libvirt: failed to open rbd volumes/volume-9c1f: Permission denied ceph client.nova lacks access to pool volumes The new host had a different Ceph key that lacked permission on the volumes pool. Once the capabilities were corrected and the secret defined on the host, the attach worked. The team added the Ceph credentials to the host build checks. Common mistakes Treating snapshots as backups Resetting volume states without checking the backend Converting images on every boot Ignoring pool capacity"},"openstack/horizon-heat-and-openstack-automation":{"html":"\u003cp>OpenStack exposes every capability through REST APIs. The dashboard, command line, SDKs and orchestration tools are different clients of the same interface, and choosing the right one makes work repeatable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>CLI and SDK basics with application credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Heat templates and stacks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Terraform with the OpenStack provider\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When the dashboard is the right tool\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli-and-credentials\">CLI and credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"export OS_CLOUD=prod            # reads clouds.yaml\nopenstack server list --all-projects --status ERROR\nopenstack application credential create ci-deploy --role member\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> OS_CLOUD=prod            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># reads clouds.yaml\u003c/span>\nopenstack server list --all-projects --status ERROR\nopenstack application credential create ci-deploy --role member\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>application credentials\u003c/strong> for automation instead of a user password. They can be limited and revoked without changing the person&#39;s account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"python-sdk\">Python SDK\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import openstack\nconn = openstack.connect(cloud=&quot;prod&quot;)\nfor s in conn.compute.servers(status=&quot;ERROR&quot;):\n    print(s.name, s.fault[&quot;message&quot;] if s.fault else &quot;&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> openstack\nconn = openstack.connect(cloud=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> conn.compute.servers(status=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ERROR&quot;\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(s.name, s.fault[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;message&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> s.fault \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"heat\">Heat\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"heat_template_version: 2021-04-16\nparameters:\n  image: {type: string, default: jammy-2026-11}\n  flavor: {type: string, default: m1.medium}\nresources:\n  net:\n    type: OS::Neutron::Net\n  subnet:\n    type: OS::Neutron::Subnet\n    properties: {network: {get_resource: net}, cidr: 10.50.0.0/24}\n  web:\n    type: OS::Nova::Server\n    properties:\n      image: {get_param: image}\n      flavor: {get_param: flavor}\n      networks: [{network: {get_resource: net}}]\noutputs:\n  web_ip:\n    value: {get_attr: [web, first_address]}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">heat_template_version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2021-04-16\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">parameters:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">string\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">default:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">jammy-2026-11\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">flavor:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">string\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">default:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">m1.medium\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">net:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">OS::Neutron::Net\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subnet:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">OS::Neutron::Subnet\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">properties:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">network:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">get_resource:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">net\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cidr:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/24\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">web:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">OS::Nova::Server\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">properties:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">get_param:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">image\u003c/span>}\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">flavor:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">get_param:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">flavor\u003c/span>}\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networks:\u003c/span> [{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">network:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">get_resource:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">net\u003c/span>}}]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">outputs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">web_ip:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">get_attr:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">first_address\u003c/span>]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack stack create -t web.yaml demo\nopenstack stack event list demo\nopenstack stack delete demo --yes --wait\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openstack stack create -t web.yaml demo\nopenstack stack event list demo\nopenstack stack delete demo --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">yes\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wait\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>stack\u003c/strong> groups resources that are created and deleted together. Heat tracks dependencies and rolls back on failure. Terraform offers similar declarative control with a shared workflow across clouds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dashboard\">Dashboard\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Horizon is useful for exploration, quotas and showing topology to new users. Do not rely on it for repeatable changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-lab-nobody-could-rebuild\">Incident: The lab nobody could rebuild\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A hand-built environment was lost with its documentation.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">project: qa-lab\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">14 servers, 3 networks, 6 volumes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">created by manual clicks, stack: none\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A quota cleanup removed some of the lab&#39;s resources and no one remembered how they were configured. After rebuilding it as a Heat stack, the team could recreate the environment in minutes and review changes as code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hand-built environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using personal passwords in automation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping stack events when debugging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving test stacks running forever\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cli-and-credentials","text":"CLI and credentials"},{"id":"python-sdk","text":"Python SDK"},{"id":"heat","text":"Heat"},{"id":"dashboard","text":"Dashboard"},{"id":"incident-the-lab-nobody-could-rebuild","text":"Incident: The lab nobody could rebuild"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack Heat documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/heat/latest/"},{"t":"OpenStack SDK documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/openstacksdk/latest/"}],"text":"OpenStack exposes every capability through REST APIs. The dashboard, command line, SDKs and orchestration tools are different clients of the same interface, and choosing the right one makes work repeatable. What you will learn CLI and SDK basics with application credentials Heat templates and stacks Terraform with the OpenStack provider When the dashboard is the right tool CLI and credentials export OS_CLOUD=prod # reads clouds.yaml openstack server list --all-projects --status ERROR openstack application credential create ci-deploy --role member Use application credentials for automation instead of a user password. They can be limited and revoked without changing the person&#39;s account. Python SDK import openstack conn = openstack.connect(cloud= \"prod\" ) for s in conn.compute.servers(status= \"ERROR\" ): print (s.name, s.fault[ \"message\" ] if s.fault else \"\" ) Heat heat_template_version: 2021-04-16 parameters: image: { type: string , default: jammy-2026-11 } flavor: { type: string , default: m1.medium } resources: net: type: OS::Neutron::Net subnet: type: OS::Neutron::Subnet properties: { network: { get_resource: net }, cidr: 10.50 .0 .0 /24 } web: type: OS::Nova::Server properties: image: { get_param: image } flavor: { get_param: flavor } networks: [{ network: { get_resource: net }}] outputs: web_ip: value: { get_attr: [ web , first_address ]} openstack stack create -t web.yaml demo openstack stack event list demo openstack stack delete demo -- yes -- wait A stack groups resources that are created and deleted together. Heat tracks dependencies and rolls back on failure. Terraform offers similar declarative control with a shared workflow across clouds. Dashboard Horizon is useful for exploration, quotas and showing topology to new users. Do not rely on it for repeatable changes. Incident: The lab nobody could rebuild Incident A hand-built environment was lost with its documentation. project: qa-lab 14 servers, 3 networks, 6 volumes created by manual clicks, stack: none A quota cleanup removed some of the lab&#39;s resources and no one remembered how they were configured. After rebuilding it as a Heat stack, the team could recreate the environment in minutes and review changes as code. Common mistakes Hand-built environments Using personal passwords in automation Skipping stack events when debugging Leaving test stacks running forever"},"openstack/deploying-openstack-with-kolla-ansible":{"html":"\u003cp>Installing OpenStack service by service by hand is slow and error prone. Kolla packages each service as a container, and Kolla-Ansible deploys and manages them across your servers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The inventory and globals files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The deployment workflow and prechecks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overriding service configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reconfigure and upgrade\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"files\">Files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# multinode inventory (excerpt)\n[control]\nctl-01\nctl-02\nctl-03\n[network]\nctl-01\nctl-02\nctl-03\n[compute]\ncmp-01\ncmp-02\n[storage]\nsto-01\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># multinode inventory (excerpt)\n[control]\nctl-01\nctl-02\nctl-03\n[network]\nctl-01\nctl-02\nctl-03\n[compute]\ncmp-01\ncmp-02\n[storage]\nsto-01\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# /etc/kolla/globals.yml (excerpt)\nkolla_base_distro: ubuntu\nopenstack_release: &quot;2025.1&quot;\nnetwork_interface: eth0\nneutron_external_interface: eth1\nkolla_internal_vip_address: 10.20.0.250\nenable_cinder: &quot;yes&quot;\nenable_haproxy: &quot;yes&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># /etc/kolla/globals.yml (excerpt)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kolla_base_distro:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">openstack_release:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025.1&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">network_interface:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">eth0\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">neutron_external_interface:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">eth1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kolla_internal_vip_address:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.250\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enable_cinder:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;yes&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enable_haproxy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;yes&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>globals.yml\u003c/code> holds cluster-wide choices. \u003ccode>passwords.yml\u003c/code> holds generated secrets and must be protected and backed up. Both belong in a private repository, with secrets encrypted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workflow\">Workflow\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kolla-genpwd\nkolla-ansible -i multinode bootstrap-servers\nkolla-ansible -i multinode prechecks\nkolla-ansible -i multinode deploy\nkolla-ansible -i multinode post-deploy\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kolla-genpwd\nkolla-ansible -i multinode bootstrap-servers\nkolla-ansible -i multinode prechecks\nkolla-ansible -i multinode deploy\nkolla-ansible -i multinode post-deploy\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Prechecks\u003c/strong> catch missing interfaces, wrong addresses, port conflicts and missing packages before anything changes. Fix every failure first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"override-configuration\">Override configuration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Place files under \u003ccode>/etc/kolla/config/&lt;service&gt;/\u003c/code> to add or override service settings, for example \u003ccode>nova.conf\u003c/code>. Run \u003ccode>kolla-ansible reconfigure\u003c/code> to apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kolla-ansible -i multinode reconfigure --tags nova\ndocker ps --format 'table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}' | head\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kolla-ansible -i multinode reconfigure --tags nova\ndocker ps --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrade\">Upgrade\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Upgrade one release at a time, read the release notes, back up databases and configuration, run \u003ccode>pull\u003c/code> to fetch images ahead of time and then \u003ccode>upgrade\u003c/code>. Test the procedure in a staging cloud that mirrors production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-precheck-that-saved-the-day\">Incident: A precheck that saved the day\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Prechecks found a wrong interface name before a deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TASK [neutron : Checking neutron_external_interface]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">fatal: [ctl-02]: FAILED! interface eth1 not found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">PLAY RECAP: ctl-02 failed=1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One controller had a different network card name. The precheck failed before any change was made. The team fixed the name in the inventory host variables, added a hardware check to server provisioning and reran the deploy successfully.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping prechecks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Losing passwords.yml\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hand-editing files inside containers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading several releases at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"files","text":"Files"},{"id":"workflow","text":"Workflow"},{"id":"override-configuration","text":"Override configuration"},{"id":"upgrade","text":"Upgrade"},{"id":"incident-a-precheck-that-saved-the-day","text":"Incident: A precheck that saved the day"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kolla-Ansible documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/kolla-ansible/latest/"},{"t":"OpenStack installation guides","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/install-guide/"}],"text":"Installing OpenStack service by service by hand is slow and error prone. Kolla packages each service as a container, and Kolla-Ansible deploys and manages them across your servers. What you will learn The inventory and globals files The deployment workflow and prechecks Overriding service configuration Reconfigure and upgrade Files # multinode inventory (excerpt) [control] ctl-01 ctl-02 ctl-03 [network] ctl-01 ctl-02 ctl-03 [compute] cmp-01 cmp-02 [storage] sto-01 # /etc/kolla/globals.yml (excerpt) kolla_base_distro: ubuntu openstack_release: \"2025.1\" network_interface: eth0 neutron_external_interface: eth1 kolla_internal_vip_address: 10.20 .0 .250 enable_cinder: \"yes\" enable_haproxy: \"yes\" globals.yml holds cluster-wide choices. passwords.yml holds generated secrets and must be protected and backed up. Both belong in a private repository, with secrets encrypted. Workflow kolla-genpwd kolla-ansible -i multinode bootstrap-servers kolla-ansible -i multinode prechecks kolla-ansible -i multinode deploy kolla-ansible -i multinode post-deploy Prechecks catch missing interfaces, wrong addresses, port conflicts and missing packages before anything changes. Fix every failure first. Override configuration Place files under /etc/kolla/config/\u003cservice>/ to add or override service settings, for example nova.conf . Run kolla-ansible reconfigure to apply. kolla-ansible -i multinode reconfigure --tags nova docker ps --format &#x27;table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}&#x27; | head Upgrade Upgrade one release at a time, read the release notes, back up databases and configuration, run pull to fetch images ahead of time and then upgrade . Test the procedure in a staging cloud that mirrors production. Incident: A precheck that saved the day Incident Prechecks found a wrong interface name before a deploy. TASK [neutron : Checking neutron_external_interface] fatal: [ctl-02]: FAILED! interface eth1 not found PLAY RECAP: ctl-02 failed=1 One controller had a different network card name. The precheck failed before any change was made. The team fixed the name in the inventory host variables, added a hardware check to server provisioning and reran the deploy successfully. Common mistakes Skipping prechecks Losing passwords.yml Hand-editing files inside containers Upgrading several releases at once"},"openstack/openstack-monitoring-and-capacity-planning":{"html":"\u003cp>OpenStack has many services with many failure modes, and users only see the symptoms: slow API calls, instances that never become active and volumes that hang. A small set of signals covers most of them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Control plane signals: APIs, queue, database and Ceph\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compute capacity and overcommit metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts that predict user-visible failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forecasting capacity and setting quotas\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-plane\">Control plane\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>API health:\u003c/strong> request latency and 5xx rates per service, through HAProxy or exporters.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Message queue:\u003c/strong> RabbitMQ queue depth, unacked messages and consumers. Growing queues mean a stuck service.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Database:\u003c/strong> connections, slow queries and replication state of Galera.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Service heartbeats:\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>openstack compute service list\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>network agent list\u003c/code> show down services.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ceph:\u003c/strong> health status, near-full pools and slow operations.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack compute service list --long | grep -v &quot; up &quot;\nopenstack network agent list | grep -v &quot;:-)&quot;\nrabbitmqctl list_queues name messages consumers | sort -k2 -nr | head\nceph -s\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openstack compute service list --long | grep -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot; up &quot;\u003c/span>\nopenstack network agent list | grep -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:-)&quot;\u003c/span>\nrabbitmqctl list_queues name messages consumers | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -k2 -nr | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nceph -s\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity\">Capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack hypervisor stats show\nopenstack hypervisor list --long -c &quot;Hypervisor Hostname&quot; -c &quot;vCPUs Used&quot; -c &quot;vCPUs&quot; -c &quot;Memory MB Used&quot; -c &quot;Memory MB&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openstack hypervisor stats show\nopenstack hypervisor list --long -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hypervisor Hostname&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vCPUs Used&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vCPUs&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Memory MB Used&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Memory MB&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Track vCPU and memory used against capacity per host aggregate, the \u003cstrong>overcommit ratio\u003c/strong> and the number of instances of each flavor that could still be scheduled. Also track storage pools, floating IPs and network ports, because they run out too.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"alerts\">Alerts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alert on service down for more than a few minutes, RabbitMQ queues rising, API error rates, Ceph warnings, compute hosts above a utilization threshold and failed instance builds. Keep a dashboard of \u003cstrong>time to fit\u003c/strong> the standard flavors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan\">Plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use growth trends and upcoming projects to forecast when capacity runs out, and account for procurement lead time. Set project quotas so that the sum is consistent with the overcommit you accept, and review them regularly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-instances-stuck-in-scheduling\">Incident: Instances stuck in scheduling\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A swollen message queue slowed every operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">rabbitmq: conductor queue messages 412000, consumers 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">instances in BUILD for over 20 minutes: 311\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nova-conductor service: down since 02:14\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A conductor service had crashed, and requests piled up in its queue. Once restarted and the queue drained, builds resumed. The team added alerts on queue depth and consumers, and an automatic restart with a page for repeated crashes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Watching only whether APIs respond\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on queue depth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring storage and network capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quotas that exceed physical capacity by a lot\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"control-plane","text":"Control plane"},{"id":"capacity","text":"Capacity"},{"id":"alerts","text":"Alerts"},{"id":"plan","text":"Plan"},{"id":"incident-instances-stuck-in-scheduling","text":"Incident: Instances stuck in scheduling"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenStack monitoring","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/operations-guide/ops-monitoring.html"},{"t":"Prometheus documentation","u":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}],"text":"OpenStack has many services with many failure modes, and users only see the symptoms: slow API calls, instances that never become active and volumes that hang. A small set of signals covers most of them. What you will learn Control plane signals: APIs, queue, database and Ceph Compute capacity and overcommit metrics Alerts that predict user-visible failure Forecasting capacity and setting quotas Control plane API health: request latency and 5xx rates per service, through HAProxy or exporters. Message queue: RabbitMQ queue depth, unacked messages and consumers. Growing queues mean a stuck service. Database: connections, slow queries and replication state of Galera. Service heartbeats: openstack compute service list and network agent list show down services. Ceph: health status, near-full pools and slow operations. openstack compute service list --long | grep -v \" up \" openstack network agent list | grep -v \":-)\" rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages consumers | sort -k2 -nr | head ceph -s Capacity openstack hypervisor stats show openstack hypervisor list --long -c \"Hypervisor Hostname\" -c \"vCPUs Used\" -c \"vCPUs\" -c \"Memory MB Used\" -c \"Memory MB\" Track vCPU and memory used against capacity per host aggregate, the overcommit ratio and the number of instances of each flavor that could still be scheduled. Also track storage pools, floating IPs and network ports, because they run out too. Alerts Alert on service down for more than a few minutes, RabbitMQ queues rising, API error rates, Ceph warnings, compute hosts above a utilization threshold and failed instance builds. Keep a dashboard of time to fit the standard flavors. Plan Use growth trends and upcoming projects to forecast when capacity runs out, and account for procurement lead time. Set project quotas so that the sum is consistent with the overcommit you accept, and review them regularly. Incident: Instances stuck in scheduling Incident A swollen message queue slowed every operation. rabbitmq: conductor queue messages 412000, consumers 0 instances in BUILD for over 20 minutes: 311 nova-conductor service: down since 02:14 A conductor service had crashed, and requests piled up in its queue. Once restarted and the queue drained, builds resumed. The team added alerts on queue depth and consumers, and an automatic restart with a page for repeated crashes. Common mistakes Watching only whether APIs respond No alert on queue depth Ignoring storage and network capacity Quotas that exceed physical capacity by a lot"}});